# Smart Sea Belt

## Integrated Wearable Marine Safety System

Smart Sea Belt is a prototype marine safety system designed to support fishermen and other users exposed to water-related risks.

The system integrates wearable sensing, short-range communication, long-range communication, emergency alerts, flotation support, and a rudder-diversion concept into a connected safety architecture.

## Project Objective

The project aims to reduce the time between detecting an emergency and initiating an appropriate safety response.

## System Architecture

```text
Smart Watch
     │
     │ BLE
     ▼
Boat Hub
     │
     │ LoRa
     ▼
Base Unit
     │
     ▼
Monitoring & Alerts
```

## Main Components

### Smart Watch

The wearable unit is designed to monitor:

- Heart rate
- Motion
- Emergency SOS activation
- Location-related information where supported

The Smart Watch uses an ESP32-C3 as its controller and communicates with the Boat Hub using Bluetooth Low Energy.

### Boat Hub

The Boat Hub acts as the central processing and communication unit.

Main functions include:

- Receiving Smart Watch data
- Processing emergency information
- GPS-based location awareness
- Long-range communication
- Emergency alert processing
- Interface for the rudder mechanism

### Base Unit

The Base Unit receives information from the Boat Hub and provides an additional monitoring and alert endpoint.

## Communication

| Communication | Purpose |
|---|---|
| BLE | Smart Watch → Boat Hub |
| LoRa | Boat Hub → Base Unit |
| GPS | Location information |
| Future IoT | Remote monitoring and mobile application |

## Emergency Functions

The prototype architecture supports:

- SOS emergency activation
- Motion/fall-related event detection
- Heart-rate monitoring
- Emergency alerts
- Flotation deployment
- Rudder diversion concept
- Location-aware emergency communication

## Prototype Testing

The Smart Sea Belt prototype was tested in a controlled backwater environment.

Testing activities included evaluation of the prototype's major components and emergency-response functions.

Detailed test information and supporting evidence are available in the [`results`](./results/) directory.

